   "stem": {
      "text": "Find \\( \\lim_{x \\to -3} \\dfrac{x^2 + 2x - 3}{x^2 + 4x + 3} \\)."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": 2
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "At x = -3 the numerator is 9 - 6 - 3 = 0 and the denominator is 9 - 12 + 3 = 0, so the quotient is zero over zero and must be rewritten.",
         "mathjson": 0
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Factor both parts: x^2 + 2x - 3 = (x + 3)(x - 1) and x^2 + 4x + 3 = (x + 3)(x + 1).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            [
               "Multiply",
               [
                  "Add",
                  "x",
                  3
               ],
               [
                  "Subtract",
                  "x",
                  1
               ]
            ],
            [
               "Multiply",
               [
                  "Add",
                  "x",
                  3
               ],
               [
                  "Add",
                  "x",
                  1
               ]
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "For x not equal to -3 the common factor x + 3 divides out, leaving (x - 1)/(x + 1).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            [
               "Subtract",
               "x",
               1
            ],
            [
               "Add",
               "x",
               1
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "The simplified quotient is continuous at -3, so the limit is (-3 - 1)/(-3 + 1) = -4/(-2) = 2.",
         "mathjson": 2
      }
   ],
